[h1]Flavorium Universalis v0.3.11: The Age of Revolutions Reckoning[/h1]

[i]Compatible with EU5 1.3.* | Multiplayer synchronized[/i]

[hr][/hr]

The Overlord-Subject Bond system has tracked five dimensions of every subject relationship since v0.2, and the Age of Revolutions payoffs were where all of that was supposed to land. They landed weakly. This release rebuilds them: the payoffs now read the whole record rather than a single number, they resolve into three outcome bands instead of one, and they connect to vanilla's actual revolution machinery instead of running beside it.

[h2]Payoffs now reach vanilla's revolution[/h2]

Previously a two-century loyal colonial relationship was worth a modest subject-loyalty modifier and changed nothing about whether the revolution came for you. Both poles now write to the systems EU5 already uses.

[list]
[*] [b]Loyal outcomes suppress it:[/b] estate satisfaction and stability grants that move the conditions [i]revolution_disaster[/i] and [i]aspiration_for_liberty[/i] actually check. The deepest outcomes block the revolution disaster outright for 25 years and grant [b]The Crown That Held[/b].
[*] [b]Hostile outcomes bolster it:[/b] the worst relationships set the same flag vanilla sets when a court refuses reform, which substantially loosens the revolution disaster's start conditions. The most extreme ones spawn a sympathy revolt in your own capital region.
[*] [b]A march lord can become the revolution:[/b] cut a deeply estranged military march loose and it leaves as a revolutionary republic on your border, with the army you trained.
[/list]

No vanilla file is overridden. All of this is written through vanilla's own variables and effects.

[h2]Three outcome bands instead of one[/h2]

Every payoff is now graded 1 to 3 from the weaker of its two tracked dimensions, escalated by the worst the relationship ever got and by a standing record of fighting you. A merely good relationship gets a local, proportionate outcome. An exceptional one changes the map. Descriptions, options and payload magnitudes all scale with the band.

[h2]Your subjects remember what you actually did[/h2]

The bond chains accumulate around thirty memory flags across a campaign, one per decision you made. The payoffs used to read two of them, and one of those changed nothing.

[list]
[*] [b]24 memory flags now count.[/b] A granted colonial assembly, a funded fortress, a ward raised at court, a suppressed scholar, a forced conversion, a denied merchant: each carries weight, and together they can move a payoff a full band in either direction.
[*] [b]The events say why.[/b] Payoffs narrate the specific history that produced them. A colony revolts differently when the pamphlets are being written by the scholar you silenced.
[*] [b]The ward chain pays off.[/b] Bringing a march lord's heir to court previously changed one paragraph of text. A loyal ward now returns as a commander for your army; a hostile one leads the revolt against you.
[/list]

[h2]The payoff gates were unreachable, and are not now[/h2]

Long-term memory only updated on a sweep armed 70 years after a subject was acquired, then every 60 to 80 years. Any subject taken after roughly 1665 had never swept by the time the Age of Revolutions read the result, so its long-term average was zero and the gates could not be cleared.

[list]
[*] [b]Sweeps now run every 25 to 35 years,[/b] starting 25 years in.
[*] [b]Young relationships are judged on current standing[/b] rather than on a record that does not exist yet, and established ones on their record.
[*] [b]A minimum tenure guard[/b] stops a subject acquired in 1730 receiving a verdict about a generation of good choices.
[/list]

[h2]Pacing and coverage[/h2]

[list]
[*] [b]One payoff at a time.[/b] All payoffs previously fired from an unpaced yearly list, so a wide empire could resolve most of its subject relationships in the single year the age turned over. They now run through one dispatcher: at most one per four years, starting 5 to 15 years after the age begins, worst relationships first.
[*] [b]Puppet states have an ending.[/b] They had a full seven-event chain and no terminal event. Now they have both a survival and a collapse outcome.
[*] [b]Imperial Council members have one too.[/b] Three separate payoffs promote subjects into that status, which previously led nowhere.
[*] [b]The cultural dimension finally matters.[/b] Assimilation, forced conversion and institution flow have always fed a cultural score that no payoff read. Two new events resolve it.
[/list]

[h2]Fixes[/h2]

[list]
[*] The crown dependency defection payoff required two conditions that were meant to be alternatives, roughly halving how often it fired.
[*] Released subjects were not type-checked, so a freed crown dependency could trigger the loyal colonial nation event.
[*] Payoff effects were applied before the player chose an option, leaving the visible choice as decoration on an outcome already committed. Options now carry the payload and differ meaningfully.
[*] The march loyalty event's two options had identical effects.
[*] The palatinate interregnum branch was a pure penalty with no way to decline it. Settling the debt now buys more than ignoring it.
[*] Several concession options paid gold to write a bond score that nothing would ever read again.
[/list]

[h2]Localization[/h2]

French, German and Spanish text is updated for all new and rewritten events.
